  "visualLines": [
    {
      "lineId": 0,
      "y": 746.64,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "NIST SP 800-63B DIGITAL IDENTITY GUIDELINES:",
      "itemIndexes": [
        0,
        1,
        2
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 1,
      "y": 735.12,
      "height": 10.08,
      "text": "AUTHENTICATION & LIFECYCLE MANAGEMENT",
      "itemIndexes": [
        4
      ],
      "firstX": 351.36
    },
    {
      "lineId": 2,
      "y": 708.96,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "Approved cryptographic algorithms SHALL be used to establish verifier impersonation",
      "itemIndexes": [
        10
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 3,
      "y": 695.28,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "resistance where it is required. Keys used for this purpose SHALL provide at least the minimum",
      "itemIndexes": [
        11
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 4,
      "y": 681.36,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "security strength specified in the latest revision of SP 800-131A (112 bits as of the date of this",
      "itemIndexes": [
        12,
        13,
        14,
        15,
        16
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 5,
      "y": 667.44,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "publication).",
      "itemIndexes": [
        17
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 6,
      "y": 641.76,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "One example of a verifier impersonation-resistant authentication protocol is client-authenticated",
      "itemIndexes": [
        19
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 7,
      "y": 628.08,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "TLS, because the client signs the authenticator output along with earlier messages from the",
      "itemIndexes": [
        20
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 8,
      "y": 613.92,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "protocol that are unique to the particular TLS connection being negotiated.",
      "itemIndexes": [
        21
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 9,
      "y": 588.48,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "Authenticators that involve the manual entry of an authenticator output, such as out-of-band and",
      "itemIndexes": [
        23
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 10,
      "y": 574.56,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "OTP authenticators, SHALL NOT be considered verifier impersonation-resistant because the",
      "itemIndexes": [
        24
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 11,
      "y": 562.68,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "This publication is available free of charge from: https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.SP.800-63b manual entry does not bind the authenticator output to the specific session being authenticated.",
      "itemIndexes": [
        8,
        25
      ],
      "firstX": 20.88
    },
    {
      "lineId": 12,
      "y": 546.96,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "In a MitM attack, an impostor verifier could replay the OTP authenticator output to the verifier",
      "itemIndexes": [
        26
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 13,
      "y": 533.04,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "and successfully authenticate.",
      "itemIndexes": [
        27
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 14,
      "y": 508.08,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "5.2.6 Verifier-CSP Communications",
      "itemIndexes": [
        29,
        30,
        31
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 15,
      "y": 482.64,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "In situations where the verifier and CSP are separate entities (as shown by the dotted line in SP",
      "itemIndexes": [
        33,
        34,
        35
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 16,
      "y": 468.96,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "800-63-3 Figure 4-1), communications between the verifier and CSP SHALL occur through a",
      "itemIndexes": [
        36,
        37,
        38
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 17,
      "y": 455.04,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "mutually-authenticated secure channel (such as a client-authenticated TLS connection) using",
      "itemIndexes": [
        39
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 18,
      "y": 441.12,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "approved cryptography.",
      "itemIndexes": [
        40
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 19,
      "y": 416.16,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "5.2.7 Verifier-Compromise Resistance",
      "itemIndexes": [
        42,
        43,
        44
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 20,
      "y": 390.96,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "Use of some types of authenticators requires that the verifier store a copy of the authenticator",
      "itemIndexes": [
        46
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 21,
      "y": 377.04,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "secret. For example, an OTP authenticator (described in Section 5.1.4 ) requires that the verifier",
      "itemIndexes": [
        47,
        48,
        49,
        50
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 22,
      "y": 363.36,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "independently generate the authenticator output for comparison against the value sent by the",
      "itemIndexes": [
        51
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 23,
      "y": 349.44,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "claimant. Because of the potential for the verifier to be compromised and stored secrets stolen,",
      "itemIndexes": [
        52
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 24,
      "y": 335.76,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "authentication protocols that do not require the verifier to persistently store secrets that could be",
      "itemIndexes": [
        53
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 25,
      "y": 321.84,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "used for authentication are considered stronger, and are described herein as being verifier",
      "itemIndexes": [
        54,
        55,
        56
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 26,
      "y": 308.16,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "compromise resistant . Note that such verifiers are not resistant to all attacks. A verifier could be",
      "itemIndexes": [
        57,
        58
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 27,
      "y": 294.24,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "compromised in a different way, such as being manipulated into always accepting a particular",
      "itemIndexes": [
        59
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 28,
      "y": 280.32,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "authenticator output.",
      "itemIndexes": [
        60
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 29,
      "y": 254.4,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "Verifier compromise resistance can be achieved in different ways, for example:",
      "itemIndexes": [
        62
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 30,
      "y": 228,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "• Use a cryptographic authenticator that requires the verifier store a public key",
      "itemIndexes": [
        64,
        65,
        66
      ],
      "firstX": 90
    },
    {
      "lineId": 31,
      "y": 214.32,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "corresponding to a private key held by the authenticator.",
      "itemIndexes": [
        67
      ],
      "firstX": 108
    },
    {
      "lineId": 32,
      "y": 199.44,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "• Store the expected authenticator output in hashed form. This method can be used with",
      "itemIndexes": [
        69,
        70,
        71
      ],
      "firstX": 90
    },
    {
      "lineId": 33,
      "y": 185.52,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "some look-up secret authenticators (described in Section 5.1.2 ), for example.",
      "itemIndexes": [
        72,
        73,
        74,
        75
      ],
      "firstX": 108
    },
    {
      "lineId": 34,
      "y": 160.08,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "To be considered verifier compromise resistant, public keys stored by the verifier SHALL be",
      "itemIndexes": [
        77
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 35,
      "y": 146.16,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "associated with the use of approved cryptographic algorithms and SHALL provide at least the",
      "itemIndexes": [
        78
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 36,
      "y": 132.48,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "minimum security strength specified in the latest revision of SP 800-131A (112 bits as of the",
      "itemIndexes": [
        79,
        80,
        81,
        82,
        83
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 37,
      "y": 118.32,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "date of this publication).",
      "itemIndexes": [
        84
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 38,
      "y": 92.88,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "Other verifier compromise resistant secrets SHALL use approved hash algorithms and the",
      "itemIndexes": [
        86
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
      "lineId": 39,
      "y": 78.96,
      "height": 12,
      "text": "underlying secrets SHALL have at least the minimum security strength specified in the latest",
      "itemIndexes": [
        87
      ],
      "firstX": 72
    },
    {
